          <mxGeometry height="30" width="369" x="-74" y="2300" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="kZwhHGzqvlMfPyiFF4_c-62" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="Q1. How to deal with scenario where a user is following 10K users? Fetching list of all these followers and then posts of these folks from DB would be a very slow operation&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;A1. Classic pattern where we want to make reads cheaper by doing some pre-computation or making writes heavier&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Whenever a new post is created by user U1, look up all the folllowers of U1 (U2, U3,...)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;And then store in a precomputed feed table, the list if posts this user can see&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Example:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;User2 -&amp;gt; [Post1]&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Benefit: When user2 wants to see their feed, we have already pre computed the lists of posts to be shown and thus latency is lower&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Options to do this pre-computation:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;1. Sync: Whenever a new post is created, synchronously make the user wait until this post has been propagated to all the followers. This would be problematic in nature for celebrities with 1 million + followers, as it would be very slow and increase the latency&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;2. Async: Better approach still the post creation latency won&#39;t suffer. However, there&#39;s still a problem: for celebrities having 100M users, doing 100M writes to the precomputed feed is still computationally and monetarily expensive (async nature only buys time, it doesn&#39;t solve the expensive aspect)&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Hence, a hybrid approach is suggested :&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;1. Non-celebrity accounts having low to mid followers: Async workers write to the pre computed feed table&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;2. Celebrity accountd with high follower count: Use the DB subqeuery to first fetch the list of all followers followed by their posts (how we were doing earlier)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Q2. Let&#39;s say a celebrity with 100M followers just posted a new post, and this post has gone viral. Since a lot of read requests are being issued for this post, it it putting stress on one of the Cassandra/DynamoDB causing hot partition problem&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="790" width="376" x="-11" y="2373" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="kZwhHGzqvlMfPyiFF4_c-64" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="Updated HLD diagram after deep dives:" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="30" width="294" x="-103" y="3391" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="kZwhHGzqvlMfPyiFF4_c-65" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="A2. Use Redis Cache to cache the popular post IDs. It would have dual advantage:&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;1.&amp;nbsp; Due to cache hit, the IOPS on DB would reduce significantly&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;2. Low latency data fetch&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="30" width="353" x="-11" y="3190" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="kZwhHGzqvlMfPyiFF4_c-66" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="Q3. But if we are leveraging a single Redis node/cluster with consistent hashing, we can again run into the same hot partition problem due to a viral post being located on a single shard&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;A3. Instead of creating a Redis cluster with sharding, have multiple uncoordinated Redis nodes - think of them like standalone Redis instances. The read workload from the feed service will be distributed among these multiple Redis nodes, thus avoiding hot partition problem&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="30" width="420" x="-6" y="3266" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="kZwhHGzqvlMfPyiFF4_c-67" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;" value="Client" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="60" width="120" x="-99" y="3693" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
